1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,716
Eight weeks ago, Jay planted one tomato

2
00:00:02,807 --> 00:00:05,130
seed in a pot of dry soil,

3
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:06,716
and he weighed the soil first.

4
00:00:06,943 --> 00:00:09,584
Now the plant comes up past his knee.

5
00:00:09,861 --> 00:00:12,830
He pulls it out, dries the soil again,

6
00:00:12,867 --> 00:00:14,415
and puts it back on the scale.

7
00:00:14,641 --> 00:00:16,981
It's lost only a few grams,

8
00:00:17,031 --> 00:00:18,716
about half a teaspoon of salt.

9
00:00:19,823 --> 00:00:21,811
So where did all that plant come from?

10
00:00:22,138 --> 00:00:24,528
By the end of this video you'll be able

11
00:00:24,553 --> 00:00:25,353
to answer that.

12
00:00:25,415 --> 00:00:27,849
You'll also know the six elements almost

13
00:00:27,912 --> 00:00:30,037
every living thing is built from,

14
00:00:30,075 --> 00:00:32,075
the four big families of molecules they

15
00:00:32,150 --> 00:00:34,867
make, and the two reactions that build those

16
00:00:34,928 --> 00:00:36,830
molecules and take them apart.

17
00:00:38,067 --> 00:00:40,075
Your body is made of atoms,

18
00:00:40,113 --> 00:00:42,264
and every one of them came from somewhere

19
00:00:42,339 --> 00:00:42,908
else.

20
00:00:42,928 --> 00:00:45,583
Once you see how they snap together,

21
00:00:45,603 --> 00:00:47,849
the rest of this unit gets a lot easier.

22
00:00:51,349 --> 00:00:53,764
An element is made of only one kind of atom,

23
00:00:53,824 --> 00:00:55,575
like carbon or oxygen.

24
00:00:55,696 --> 00:00:57,915
What makes an atom carbon is its number

25
00:00:57,965 --> 00:00:59,122
of protons.

26
00:00:59,210 --> 00:01:01,234
Every carbon atom has six,

27
00:01:01,254 --> 00:01:04,028
and around the nucleus it has six electrons.

28
00:01:04,254 --> 00:01:07,952
Atoms bond by sharing or trading those electrons.

29
00:01:08,084 --> 00:01:10,518
But the number of neutrons can vary.

30
00:01:10,624 --> 00:01:14,140
Most carbon has six, a little has seven,

31
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:16,179
and a tiny trace has eight.

32
00:01:16,304 --> 00:01:19,500
Those are isotopes, named by protons plus

33
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:22,367
neutrons: carbon twelve, thirteen,

34
00:01:22,405 --> 00:01:23,273
and fourteen.

35
00:01:23,469 --> 00:01:26,367
Some isotopes are unstable and give off

36
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:28,103
radiation as they break down,

37
00:01:28,133 --> 00:01:29,764
like phosphorus thirty two.

38
00:01:29,892 --> 00:01:32,556
Chemistry depends on electrons,

39
00:01:32,594 --> 00:01:34,895
and an isotope has the same electrons as

40
00:01:34,915 --> 00:01:35,952
the normal atom.

41
00:01:36,179 --> 00:01:39,273
So a cell uses radioactive phosphorus just

42
00:01:39,303 --> 00:01:41,764
like normal phosphorus, and scientists can

43
00:01:41,829 --> 00:01:43,801
follow where those atoms end up.

44
00:01:43,922 --> 00:01:46,141
That's called a tracer.

45
00:01:46,227 --> 00:01:48,481
Living things can't make atoms.

46
00:01:48,582 --> 00:01:50,971
Every atom in that tomato plant was taken

47
00:01:51,021 --> 00:01:52,481
in from its surroundings.

48
00:01:52,682 --> 00:01:55,575
In photosynthesis, a plant uses the energy

49
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:58,216
in light to build sugar out of carbon dioxide

50
00:01:58,254 --> 00:02:00,707
and water, and it releases oxygen.

51
00:02:00,918 --> 00:02:03,424
Then in cellular respiration,

52
00:02:03,478 --> 00:02:05,915
nearly every cell, the plant's own cells

53
00:02:06,009 --> 00:02:08,783
too, breaks that sugar down with oxygen.

54
00:02:08,918 --> 00:02:11,877
That releases carbon dioxide and water,

55
00:02:11,915 --> 00:02:13,990
and the energy gets captured in a molecule

56
00:02:14,044 --> 00:02:15,500
called ATP.

57
00:02:15,776 --> 00:02:17,688
Atoms go around in a loop.

58
00:02:17,796 --> 00:02:19,122
Energy doesn't.

59
00:02:19,248 --> 00:02:21,462
It comes in as light and is stored for a

60
00:02:21,525 --> 00:02:23,198
while in chemical bonds.

61
00:02:23,323 --> 00:02:25,990
Every time it's used, some of it turns into

62
00:02:26,066 --> 00:02:28,783
heat, and no living thing can turn heat

63
00:02:28,858 --> 00:02:30,330
back into fuel.

64
00:02:30,937 --> 00:02:33,764
So matter cycles, and energy flows through.

65
00:02:33,952 --> 00:02:36,103
Which solves Jay's puzzle.

66
00:02:36,311 --> 00:02:38,971
The plant wasn't built out of the soil.

67
00:02:39,107 --> 00:02:41,537
Most of it was built out of carbon dioxide

68
00:02:41,583 --> 00:02:43,273
from the air, and water.

69
00:02:43,399 --> 00:02:46,594
Those few grams the soil lost were minerals

70
00:02:46,632 --> 00:02:47,726
the roots pulled up.

71
00:02:47,952 --> 00:02:49,915
Your turn.

72
00:02:50,141 --> 00:02:52,632
Glucose, the sugar plants make,

73
00:02:52,682 --> 00:02:55,500
is C six H twelve O six.

74
00:02:55,613 --> 00:02:57,915
The small numbers count the atoms.

75
00:02:58,009 --> 00:03:00,330
How many atoms are in one molecule,

76
00:03:00,405 --> 00:03:02,366
and where does a plant get the carbon and

77
00:03:02,386 --> 00:03:03,198
the hydrogen?

78
00:03:08,862 --> 00:03:11,692
Six carbon, twelve hydrogen,

79
00:03:11,749 --> 00:03:15,013
and six oxygen, so twenty four atoms.

80
00:03:15,201 --> 00:03:17,729
The carbon comes from carbon dioxide in

81
00:03:17,749 --> 00:03:20,371
the air, and the hydrogen comes from water.

82
00:03:23,871 --> 00:03:25,833
About twenty five elements show up in living

83
00:03:25,899 --> 00:03:28,173
things, but six of them make up roughly

84
00:03:28,249 --> 00:03:30,664
ninety eight percent of a cell's mass.

85
00:03:30,793 --> 00:03:33,909
Carbon, hydrogen, oxygen,

86
00:03:33,976 --> 00:03:36,777
nitrogen, phosphorus, and sulfur.

87
00:03:37,116 --> 00:03:39,796
A lot of students remember them as CHONPS.

88
00:03:39,977 --> 00:03:42,249
Each one has a job.

89
00:03:42,330 --> 00:03:44,928
Carbon is the skeleton of every carbon based

90
00:03:44,986 --> 00:03:45,984
molecule in you.

91
00:03:46,135 --> 00:03:48,928
Hydrogen and oxygen show up in nearly all

92
00:03:48,953 --> 00:03:50,588
of them, and in water.

93
00:03:50,758 --> 00:03:53,607
You need nitrogen for every protein and

94
00:03:53,645 --> 00:03:54,513
for DNA.

95
00:03:54,789 --> 00:03:57,079
Phosphorus goes into DNA,

96
00:03:57,249 --> 00:04:00,400
ATP, and the phospholipids in cell membranes.

97
00:04:00,550 --> 00:04:03,267
And sulfur sits in a couple of protein building

98
00:04:03,333 --> 00:04:05,909
blocks, so a lot of proteins carry it.

99
00:04:06,230 --> 00:04:09,003
Jay and his grandma plant the tomato out

100
00:04:09,029 --> 00:04:11,267
in her garden, and she tips in a scoop of

101
00:04:11,317 --> 00:04:12,117
plant food.

102
00:04:12,211 --> 00:04:15,569
The label says N, P, K.

103
00:04:15,781 --> 00:04:18,286
That's nitrogen, phosphorus,

104
00:04:18,381 --> 00:04:19,267
and potassium.

105
00:04:19,569 --> 00:04:22,813
Air is mostly nitrogen gas,

106
00:04:22,833 --> 00:04:25,305
but a tomato can't use that form,

107
00:04:25,355 --> 00:04:27,192
so it needs nitrogen from the soil.

108
00:04:28,299 --> 00:04:30,211
So the soil did matter after all,

109
00:04:30,407 --> 00:04:32,626
just not for the bulk of the plant.

110
00:04:32,867 --> 00:04:34,286
Your turn.

111
00:04:34,550 --> 00:04:37,230
Say the tomato gets plenty of light,

112
00:04:37,305 --> 00:04:40,324
air, and water, but its soil has almost

113
00:04:40,425 --> 00:04:41,456
no nitrogen.

114
00:04:41,637 --> 00:04:44,550
Does sugar itself contain nitrogen?

115
00:04:44,732 --> 00:04:46,060
Does protein?

116
00:04:46,437 --> 00:04:49,154
So which one is the plant going to struggle

117
00:04:49,204 --> 00:04:50,004
to build?

118
00:04:55,384 --> 00:04:59,083
Sugar has no nitrogen, but every protein

119
00:04:59,173 --> 00:05:01,724
does, so proteins are the problem.

120
00:05:01,932 --> 00:05:05,422
And the plant's photosynthesis runs on proteins

121
00:05:05,573 --> 00:05:08,894
and on chlorophyll, which also needs nitrogen.

122
00:05:09,196 --> 00:05:11,611
So the leaves turn yellow,

123
00:05:11,661 --> 00:05:14,479
it makes less sugar too, and it can't grow

124
00:05:14,537 --> 00:05:15,337
normally.

125
00:05:18,733 --> 00:05:20,922
Organic molecules are built on a skeleton

126
00:05:20,948 --> 00:05:23,186
of carbon, usually bonded to hydrogen.

127
00:05:23,274 --> 00:05:25,979
Water, salts, and small carbon compounds

128
00:05:26,009 --> 00:05:28,847
like carbon dioxide count as inorganic.

129
00:05:28,998 --> 00:05:31,941
Carbon is special because each carbon atom

130
00:05:31,991 --> 00:05:34,581
makes four covalent bonds,

131
00:05:34,601 --> 00:05:37,224
and a covalent bond is two atoms sharing

132
00:05:37,262 --> 00:05:38,733
a pair of electrons.

133
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:41,979
So carbons can link up into long chains,

134
00:05:42,024 --> 00:05:44,318
branched chains, and rings,

135
00:05:44,356 --> 00:05:46,507
and two carbons can share two pairs,

136
00:05:46,533 --> 00:05:47,790
which is a double bond.

137
00:05:47,941 --> 00:05:50,884
That framework is the carbon skeleton.

138
00:05:51,337 --> 00:05:54,205
A molecule of only carbon and hydrogen is

139
00:05:54,243 --> 00:05:56,922
a hydrocarbon, like the wax in a candle.

140
00:05:57,103 --> 00:06:00,317
When two atoms share electrons unevenly,

141
00:06:00,337 --> 00:06:03,186
the bond is polar, with one slightly negative

142
00:06:03,262 --> 00:06:05,601
end and one slightly positive end.

143
00:06:05,765 --> 00:06:08,922
Water is polar, and it clings to other water

144
00:06:08,990 --> 00:06:11,639
molecules and to many polar molecules through

145
00:06:11,698 --> 00:06:12,922
hydrogen bonds.

146
00:06:13,092 --> 00:06:16,394
But carbon and hydrogen share almost evenly,

147
00:06:16,444 --> 00:06:18,808
so hydrocarbons are nonpolar,

148
00:06:18,828 --> 00:06:20,394
and they don't mix with water.

149
00:06:20,639 --> 00:06:23,111
The same atoms can also be put together

150
00:06:23,161 --> 00:06:24,318
in different ways.

151
00:06:24,469 --> 00:06:28,016
Glucose and fructose are both C six H twelve

152
00:06:28,205 --> 00:06:30,960
O six, but their atoms are bonded in a different

153
00:06:31,023 --> 00:06:33,375
order, so they have different shapes,

154
00:06:33,413 --> 00:06:35,413
and fructose tastes sweeter.

155
00:06:35,549 --> 00:06:38,130
Same formula, different structure.

156
00:06:38,268 --> 00:06:40,016
Those are isomers.

157
00:06:40,228 --> 00:06:41,677
Your turn.

158
00:06:41,866 --> 00:06:44,922
Two molecules both have the formula C two

159
00:06:45,149 --> 00:06:46,281
H six O.

160
00:06:46,507 --> 00:06:49,752
One is ethanol, the alcohol in drinks,

161
00:06:49,828 --> 00:06:52,696
and the other is a gas used in spray cans.

162
00:06:52,866 --> 00:06:55,186
How can they be different molecules,

163
00:06:55,243 --> 00:06:56,545
and what's the word for them?

164
00:07:02,096 --> 00:07:03,605
They're isomers.

165
00:07:03,801 --> 00:07:06,473
Same atoms, same formula,

166
00:07:06,511 --> 00:07:08,511
but bonded in a different arrangement,

167
00:07:08,586 --> 00:07:10,020
so they behave differently.

168
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:16,916
A bare carbon skeleton is just a hydrocarbon.

169
00:07:17,037 --> 00:07:19,256
What makes molecules behave differently

170
00:07:19,357 --> 00:07:21,822
is the small clusters of atoms stuck onto

171
00:07:21,872 --> 00:07:22,305
it.

172
00:07:22,325 --> 00:07:24,237
Those are functional groups,

173
00:07:24,294 --> 00:07:26,652
and each one behaves about the same no matter

174
00:07:26,702 --> 00:07:28,237
which molecule it's on.

175
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:31,181
Hydroxyl is O H.

176
00:07:31,306 --> 00:07:33,973
It's polar, so it hydrogen bonds with water

177
00:07:34,030 --> 00:07:35,860
and helps a molecule dissolve.

178
00:07:36,026 --> 00:07:39,407
Carbonyl, a carbon double bonded to an oxygen,

179
00:07:39,457 --> 00:07:40,615
is polar too.

180
00:07:40,728 --> 00:07:43,558
You'll find both of them all over sugars.

181
00:07:43,739 --> 00:07:47,633
Next, carboxyl, C O O H.

182
00:07:47,784 --> 00:07:50,124
It's an acid, because it gives off a hydrogen

183
00:07:50,218 --> 00:07:52,011
ion, H plus.

184
00:07:52,350 --> 00:07:55,558
Amino, N H two, does the opposite.

185
00:07:55,709 --> 00:07:58,124
It's a base, because it picks one up.

186
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:01,218
Every amino acid, the building blocks of

187
00:08:01,294 --> 00:08:03,030
proteins, has both.

188
00:08:03,199 --> 00:08:05,596
And phosphate has given off its hydrogen

189
00:08:05,686 --> 00:08:08,162
ions, so it's negatively charged.

190
00:08:08,256 --> 00:08:11,181
You'll find it in DNA and ATP.

191
00:08:11,445 --> 00:08:15,483
Two sulfhydryls, S H, can bond to each other

192
00:08:15,558 --> 00:08:18,049
and link two parts of a protein together.

193
00:08:18,237 --> 00:08:21,973
And methyl, C H three, is nonpolar.

194
00:08:22,124 --> 00:08:24,916
Stick one on DNA, and it can change how

195
00:08:24,954 --> 00:08:26,426
a gene is read.

196
00:08:26,622 --> 00:08:28,162
Your turn.

197
00:08:28,256 --> 00:08:31,332
The amino acid cysteine is drawn here.

198
00:08:31,483 --> 00:08:33,822
Name its three functional groups,

199
00:08:33,860 --> 00:08:35,784
and tell me which elements a cell needs

200
00:08:35,834 --> 00:08:36,634
to make it.

201
00:08:43,116 --> 00:08:44,901
It has a sulfhydryl group,

202
00:08:45,166 --> 00:08:48,901
S H, an amino group, N H two,

203
00:08:48,996 --> 00:08:52,826
and a carboxyl group, C O O H.

204
00:08:53,128 --> 00:08:56,373
So a cell needs carbon, hydrogen,

205
00:08:56,459 --> 00:08:59,090
oxygen, nitrogen, and sulfur.

206
00:09:00,033 --> 00:09:02,335
Without sulfur, it can't make cysteine,

207
00:09:02,536 --> 00:09:04,599
or any protein that has it.

208
00:09:08,099 --> 00:09:09,835
Jay's grandma calls him in for lunch.

209
00:09:09,961 --> 00:09:12,476
Bread, a bit of butter, cheese,

210
00:09:12,496 --> 00:09:14,288
and sliced tomatoes from the garden.

211
00:09:14,484 --> 00:09:17,081
Most molecules in a cell belong to four

212
00:09:17,156 --> 00:09:19,873
families, and his lunch has all four.

213
00:09:20,081 --> 00:09:22,666
Carbohydrates are sugars and the bigger

214
00:09:22,718 --> 00:09:24,099
molecules built from them,

215
00:09:24,145 --> 00:09:25,533
like the starch in bread.

216
00:09:25,639 --> 00:09:27,872
They're made of carbon, hydrogen,

217
00:09:27,892 --> 00:09:28,692
and oxygen.

218
00:09:28,798 --> 00:09:30,892
Lipids are fats and oils,

219
00:09:30,922 --> 00:09:31,566
like butter.

220
00:09:31,586 --> 00:09:34,062
They're mostly carbon and hydrogen bonds,

221
00:09:34,107 --> 00:09:36,250
like candle wax, so they don't mix with

222
00:09:36,326 --> 00:09:36,929
water.

223
00:09:36,949 --> 00:09:39,118
The main lipids in membranes,

224
00:09:39,172 --> 00:09:41,760
phospholipids, also carry phosphorus.

225
00:09:41,911 --> 00:09:44,477
Proteins, like the ones in cheese,

226
00:09:44,515 --> 00:09:46,213
are chains of amino acids.

227
00:09:46,300 --> 00:09:48,477
Every amino acid has carbon,

228
00:09:48,535 --> 00:09:50,892
hydrogen, oxygen, and nitrogen,

229
00:09:50,967 --> 00:09:52,477
and a few have sulfur.

230
00:09:52,892 --> 00:09:56,552
Nucleic acids, like DNA, store the information

231
00:09:56,628 --> 00:09:59,798
in genes, and they use those same four plus

232
00:09:59,863 --> 00:10:00,663
phosphorus.

233
00:10:00,816 --> 00:10:04,175
The wheat, the tomato, and the cow all had

234
00:10:04,345 --> 00:10:06,515
DNA in almost every cell.

235
00:10:06,726 --> 00:10:08,703
Your turn.

236
00:10:08,772 --> 00:10:11,949
Scientists feed cells sulfur thirty five,

237
00:10:11,986 --> 00:10:13,383
a radioactive tracer.

238
00:10:13,521 --> 00:10:16,326
Which family of molecules does most of the

239
00:10:16,376 --> 00:10:20,099
radioactive sulfur show up in?

240
00:10:25,738 --> 00:10:27,311
Proteins.

241
00:10:27,688 --> 00:10:30,405
Sulfur is in a couple of the amino acids,

242
00:10:30,481 --> 00:10:33,575
and DNA, simple sugars, and fats don't have

243
00:10:33,632 --> 00:10:34,432
any.

244
00:10:37,452 --> 00:10:39,037
Most of these big molecules,

245
00:10:39,075 --> 00:10:41,528
the macromolecules, are polymers.

246
00:10:41,664 --> 00:10:43,830
Poly means many parts.

247
00:10:43,962 --> 00:10:46,660
A polymer is a long chain of small,

248
00:10:46,707 --> 00:10:48,773
similar units called monomers,

249
00:10:48,833 --> 00:10:52,396
mono meaning one, linked by covalent bonds.

250
00:10:52,459 --> 00:10:53,905
Think of a bead necklace.

251
00:10:53,999 --> 00:10:56,547
The beads are monomers, and each bit of

252
00:10:56,612 --> 00:10:57,981
string is a bond.

253
00:10:58,245 --> 00:11:00,849
A polymer can be one kind of bead repeated

254
00:11:00,909 --> 00:11:03,792
over and over, or lots of kinds in a particular

255
00:11:03,867 --> 00:11:06,584
order, where the order spells out a message.

256
00:11:06,716 --> 00:11:08,471
Biology uses both.

257
00:11:08,622 --> 00:11:11,792
Carbohydrates, proteins, and nucleic acids

258
00:11:11,849 --> 00:11:13,150
are all polymers.

259
00:11:13,867 --> 00:11:15,491
But lipids are the exception.

260
00:11:15,611 --> 00:11:18,056
They aren't chains of repeating monomers,

261
00:11:18,132 --> 00:11:19,867
so they're not true polymers.

262
00:11:20,144 --> 00:11:22,660
So how do you join two beads?

263
00:11:22,849 --> 00:11:26,056
One monomer gives up an O H from its end,

264
00:11:26,094 --> 00:11:28,924
the other gives up an H, and those combine

265
00:11:28,984 --> 00:11:31,339
into a water molecule that's released.

266
00:11:31,509 --> 00:11:34,283
The two monomers are now joined by a new

267
00:11:34,383 --> 00:11:35,566
covalent bond.

268
00:11:35,691 --> 00:11:38,132
Water out, something built.

269
00:11:38,252 --> 00:11:41,452
That's why it's called dehydration synthesis.

270
00:11:41,593 --> 00:11:43,943
Repeat it, and you get a polymer.

271
00:11:44,082 --> 00:11:46,962
Every new bond releases one water.

272
00:11:47,214 --> 00:11:50,433
So in a straight chain, n monomers release

273
00:11:50,622 --> 00:11:52,094
n minus one waters.

274
00:11:52,211 --> 00:11:55,037
Breaking a polymer is the same reaction

275
00:11:55,132 --> 00:11:56,245
run backwards.

276
00:11:56,509 --> 00:11:59,339
A water molecule gets split across the bond.

277
00:11:59,490 --> 00:12:02,810
Its H goes to one monomer and its O H to

278
00:12:02,830 --> 00:12:04,999
the other, and the bond breaks.

279
00:12:05,150 --> 00:12:08,396
That's hydrolysis, which means splitting

280
00:12:08,456 --> 00:12:09,256
with water.

281
00:12:09,396 --> 00:12:11,566
And that's what's happening to Jay's lunch

282
00:12:11,641 --> 00:12:12,471
right now.

283
00:12:12,633 --> 00:12:15,566
Digestion is mostly hydrolysis.

284
00:12:15,742 --> 00:12:18,357
Starch and proteins get cut into sugars

285
00:12:18,377 --> 00:12:20,924
and amino acids, small enough to get into

286
00:12:20,962 --> 00:12:21,749
his blood.

287
00:12:21,769 --> 00:12:25,150
Then his cells use dehydration synthesis

288
00:12:25,226 --> 00:12:27,566
to build his own polymers out of those same

289
00:12:27,641 --> 00:12:28,441
pieces.

290
00:12:29,061 --> 00:12:31,377
The bread becomes part of Jay.

291
00:12:31,448 --> 00:12:34,283
Without help, those bonds can take centuries

292
00:12:34,358 --> 00:12:35,641
or longer to break.

293
00:12:35,893 --> 00:12:39,641
So cells use enzymes, which are mostly proteins,

294
00:12:39,691 --> 00:12:41,905
to make reactions go much faster.

295
00:12:42,071 --> 00:12:45,679
Each enzyme's shape fits only its own target.

296
00:12:45,830 --> 00:12:48,396
The enzyme in your saliva that cuts starch

297
00:12:48,501 --> 00:12:50,698
does nothing to proteins.

298
00:12:50,796 --> 00:12:51,716
Your turn.

299
00:12:52,018 --> 00:12:55,037
A cell links ten sugar monomers into one

300
00:12:55,104 --> 00:12:56,169
straight chain.

301
00:12:56,358 --> 00:12:58,962
How many water molecules are released?

302
00:12:59,150 --> 00:13:02,433
And when that chain gets hydrolyzed again,

303
00:13:02,483 --> 00:13:04,849
do the ten monomers together weigh more

304
00:13:04,949 --> 00:13:06,811
or less than the chain did?

305
00:13:13,467 --> 00:13:16,607
Nine waters, one for each bond.

306
00:13:16,758 --> 00:13:19,362
And the monomers weigh MORE.

307
00:13:19,456 --> 00:13:22,645
Hydrolysis adds the atoms of one water back

308
00:13:22,770 --> 00:13:24,984
in for every bond it breaks.

309
00:13:26,626 --> 00:13:28,230
A lot of people say less,

310
00:13:28,296 --> 00:13:30,494
because they think breaking something makes

311
00:13:30,544 --> 00:13:31,344
it lighter.

312
00:13:34,673 --> 00:13:36,635
Here it all is in one picture.

313
00:13:36,776 --> 00:13:39,579
Living things can't make atoms.

314
00:13:39,654 --> 00:13:41,843
Matter cycles through photosynthesis and

315
00:13:41,899 --> 00:13:44,183
respiration, and energy flows through.

316
00:13:44,428 --> 00:13:49,315
Six elements, C H O N P S,

317
00:13:49,420 --> 00:13:51,277
make up almost all of a cell.

318
00:13:51,417 --> 00:13:53,767
Carbon builds the skeletons,

319
00:13:53,805 --> 00:13:55,805
and functional groups give molecules their

320
00:13:55,864 --> 00:13:56,528
behavior.

321
00:13:56,548 --> 00:13:59,277
Those molecules come in four families,

322
00:13:59,315 --> 00:14:01,239
and three of them are polymers,

323
00:14:01,302 --> 00:14:03,956
built by dehydration synthesis and taken

324
00:14:04,019 --> 00:14:05,466
apart by hydrolysis.

325
00:14:08,966 --> 00:14:11,003
Back on the patio, Jay leans in to look

326
00:14:11,029 --> 00:14:13,267
at his tomato plant, and he breathes out.

327
00:14:13,433 --> 00:14:15,683
Some of the carbon in that breath was in

328
00:14:15,720 --> 00:14:17,343
his lunch an hour ago.

329
00:14:17,782 --> 00:14:20,475
And some of it could end up back in a leaf.

330
00:14:20,616 --> 00:14:22,777
The notes and practice for this topic are

331
00:14:22,852 --> 00:14:24,739
free on levlprep.com.

332
00:14:24,845 --> 00:14:26,852
Next time, we'll look at the first family

333
00:14:26,953 --> 00:14:29,418
up close, carbohydrates, and why you can

334
00:14:29,456 --> 00:14:32,135
eat starch but not wood, even though wood

335
00:14:32,211 --> 00:14:34,399
is mostly built from the same sugar.

